=== modelled DRAM traffic (a MODEL, not a measurement) =====
variant               model bytes     vs ideal   note
naive_uncoalesced         5.5e+11      2730.7x
naive                     5.5e+11      2730.7x
tiled_smem               1.72e+10        85.3x
tiled_regblock           4.29e+09        21.3x
warptile_nodbuf          4.29e+09        21.3x
warptile_dbuf            4.29e+09        21.3x
warptile_vec4            4.29e+09        21.3x
This is the reuse story docs/PROFILING.md calls the most instructive
number in the project, and `ncu --metrics dram__bytes_read.sum` is what
turns it from a model into a measurement. Predict in advance: the naive
model implies 550 GB in ~79 ms = ~6967 GB/s, which is FOUR TIMES the
T4's DRAM peak -- physically impossible, so L1/L2 must already be
absorbing most of it. That impossibility is the prediction being tested.

=== step-by-step attribution ==============================
naive_uncoalesced  -> naive                3.84x     2.9% ->  11.2% of peak   [thread->output transpose (coalescing)]
naive              -> tiled_smem           1.61x    11.2% ->  18.0% of peak   [shared-memory staging]
tiled_smem         -> tiled_regblock       4.17x    18.0% ->  75.2% of peak   [register blocking]
tiled_regblock     -> warptile_nodbuf      1.00x    75.2% ->  74.9% of peak   [lane->output permutation]
warptile_nodbuf    -> warptile_dbuf        1.09x    74.9% ->  81.3% of peak   [double-buffered smem]
warptile_dbuf      -> warptile_vec4        0.99x    81.3% ->  80.7% of peak   [float4 global->smem loads]
warptile_vec4      -> cublas               1.11x    80.7% ->  89.4% of peak   [THE REMAINING GAP]

